[{:seon.task/name "an-interrupt-leaves-nothing-pending"
  :seon.task/plan "From jolt/wiki (8c50215; seen by session WIKI at its root's server, 2026-10-21; re-verify first, the code is unchanged since): after seon.root/interrupt! stopped (loop [] (recur)) with \"interrupted by root\", every later eval in that agent bounded by :timeout-ms 200 (and in its clone) answered \"interrupted by root\" in 0.3 ms instead of running to its bound. Cause as far as traced: seon.intr's pending atom is cleared only by seon.intr/clear-pending!, called from the nREPL interrupt op (seon.repl), which root's interrupt! (SIGUSR1) never sends; a set pending makes seon.intr/run start stopped. Not traced: why pending was set although an eval was running when SIGUSR1 came. Correct: an interrupt ends exactly the form it reached; with no form running it is recorded as a no-op, and nothing carries into the next form or into a clone. Deletes the pending carry-over."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.intr-test/an-interrupt-ends-only-the-form-it-reached seon.intr-test/a-clone-starts-with-nothing-pending}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-signal-wakes-a-blocked-wait"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by worktree session DUP-ROOT (task seon.agent/an-agent-ends-with-its-root), 2026-10-23; root's go for a dependency change. An agent whose main thread waits in Chez's condition-wait (a channel take, `(<!! (chan))`, a timeout) runs no signal handler until the wait ends: neither SIGIO on its socket to root (seon.intr/exit-at-eof!) nor root's SIGUSR1 (seon.root/interrupt!), so a blocked agent outlives its root unless the fork server's group kill runs, and root cannot interrupt a blocked take. Measured (sample of a forked agent on main 8a5818c): the main thread in S_condition_wait -> pthread_cond_wait. How Chez delivers a signal (c/schsig.c forward_signal_to_scheme): to the main thread only, enqueued on its tc, SIGNALINTERRUPTPENDING and SOMETHINGPENDING set; the Scheme handler runs at the next event check, when the tc's TRAP counter runs out. Measured on macOS (a C program, SIGUSR1 to a thread in pthread_cond_wait, SA_RESTART off as Chez installs it): pthread_cond_wait returns 0 after each signal (a spurious wakeup), so S_condition_wait already returns to Scheme; the caller's loop (Jolt's park) re-waits within the TRAP interval, before any event check. The smallest correct fix, in Chez (reference-code/chez, jolt/<topic>): S_condition_wait, after its wait returns, sets TRAP(tc) to 1 when a signal is pending for this thread (SIGNALINTERRUPTPENDING), as thread creation does for a pending collection: the next procedure call checks events and the handler runs before the caller can wait again. No clock, no thread, no polling: the wakeup is the signal's own. To measure after it: whether root's interrupt! (its handler sets an interrupt token, run-interruptible escapes) also stops the take, or whether Jolt's park loop, holding its counted lock when the event fires, defers the escape past the next wait (then the park loop re-checks the token before waiting: a Jolt change on jolt/<topic>). Built (root's go, 2026-10-23): the TRAP change did not suffice (measured: the handler then ran at the entry of the caller's next condition-wait, after the caller had checked its predicate), so Chez's `condition-wait` (s/prims.ss) runs the pending signal handlers on its way out, as the event handler runs them, when interrupts are enabled (reference-code/chez `jolt/signal-wakes-wait` 6799de4, mat signal-wakes-condition-wait: #f on Chez fdd1e1e, #t after). With it SIGIO's exit runs from a blocked take. Root's interrupt needed no Jolt change: Jolt's blocking `<!!` already re-checks the thread's interrupt flag after every wake (java/async.ss ac-intr-wait!, Java's Thread.interrupt), so `seon.intr`'s SIGUSR1 handler also interrupts the current thread while an eval runs, and `seon.intr/run` answers \"interrupted by root\" whenever its token was set and clears the thread's flag when the eval ends (never the next request's). Proofs: Chez's own (a mat: a signal registered with register-signal-handler, sent to the main thread waiting in condition-wait, runs its handler while the wait is outstanding); seon.agent-test/a-blocked-agent-ends-on-its-socket-eof (an agent in `(<!! (chan))` ends with its fork server gone first); seon.agent-test/root-interrupts-a-blocked-take (an eval in `(<!! (chan))` stopped by root's interrupt!, the agent goes on)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-blocked-agent-ends-on-its-socket-eof seon.agent-test/root-interrupts-a-blocked-take}}]
